{"call_id":"call_NEdlLeHmXyqCpXxVJCf5v1k1","process_id":"76466","turn_id":"019ca9c2-47ab-7f73-b32f-cdbee00a2f18","command":["/usr/bin/bash","-lc","kspec inbox list --count && kspec inbox list --newest --limit 20"],"cwd":"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"kspec inbox list --count"},{"type":"unknown","cmd":"kspec inbox list --newest --limit 20"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"124\nInbox (20 items):\n\n  01KJKY1H (8 hours ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, bug, batch, shadow]\n    Reflection follow-up: kspec batch can fail during copy-back with ENOTEMPTY on .kspec/sessions/<session-id> (observed while batching task start+note). The batch reports all subcommands succeeded, but exits non-zero and leaves shadow worktree in a temporarily broken/deleted-file state until manual recovery (e.g., git -C .kspec restore .). Improve batch copy-back robustness for session blob directories and avoid failing the batch when payload writes succeeded.\n\n  01KJKKCB (11 hours ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, tasks]\n    Reflection: add file-based payload input to task notes (e.g., kspec task note @ref --file <path> and/or --stdin). Current command only accepts positional message, which makes multiline/special-character notes brittle and conflicts with the convention to use file-based payloads for safe shell handling.\n\n  01KJKDBK (13 hours ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, testing, sessions]\n    tests/cli/session-note-limit.test.ts has reproducible timeout instability in current baseline. During this iteration: full npm test timed out at lines 34/70/104, and isolated run timed out at lines 34/104 despite rebuilding. This appears separate from ralph prompt changes and indicates session-start note-limit path/perf regression or test flake that should be stabilized.\n\n  01KJKC9T (13 hours ago by @claude) [fix, shadow, ralph]\n    Shadow branch auto-push silently swallows failures — fire-and-forget push (shadow.ts:1159-1164) uses spawn with stdio:ignore and detached:true, making divergence errors completely invisible. In a 4-hour ralph container session, 50+ commits accumulated locally with no indication pushes were failing. The push fails because host and container both commit to kspec-meta concurrently, causing divergence on the first push attempt, and every subsequent auto-push also fails silently. Fix should: (1) capture push exit code even in fire-and-forget mode (spawn with stdio:pipe, read stderr, unref after), (2) on push failure, log warning to stderr so it appears in agent output and JSON mode, (3) consider returning a result from autoCommit/shadowPush indicating push status so callers can surface it (e.g. kspec batch could include push_failed:true in response), (4) on repeated failures, escalate visibility — e.g. after 3 consecutive push failures, emit a prominent warning. The local commit always succeeds regardless — this is about VISIBILITY of sync failures, not blocking commits. Related: the merge driver handles YAML conflicts well (56/57 commits rebased cleanly), so the recovery path works — the problem is purely that nobody knows recovery is needed.\n\n  01KJK84Y (15 hours ago by @claude) [reflection, cli, tasks]\n    Reflection: fix-cycle ergonomics mismatch — task-work guidance says 're-submit' needs_work tasks, but CLI rejects kspec task submit when status is needs_work and requires explicit kspec task start first. Consider allowing direct submit from needs_work (auto-transition to in_progress) or improving error/help text to show required two-step command sequence.\n\n  01KJK5XG (15 hours ago by @claude) [reflection, testing, ralph]\n    tests/ralph.test.ts helper runRalph splits args on whitespace, so quoted values (e.g., --focus \"keep scope narrow\") break and cause misleading unknown-command/option failures. Improve helper to accept argv arrays or robust shell-like parsing for test args.\n\n  01KJK3YZ (16 hours ago by @claude) [reflection, git, automation]\n    Add commit-scope guard for automated task work: before committing, detect pre-existing staged files unrelated to the current task and prevent accidental inclusion (or require explicit acknowledgment). In this iteration, unrelated staged files were included because they were already in the index before git add on task files.\n\n  01KJJHAR (21 hours ago by @claude) [infra, cli, reflection]\n    Worktree guard escape hatch: add env var KSPEC_GUARD_OVERRIDE_DONT_USE that bypasses the guard hook for intentional recovery operations (git reset, rebase in .kspec). Name deliberately long to discourage agent use — humans-only for recovery scenarios.\n\n  01KJHAFV (1 day ago by @claude) [reflection, cli, workflow]\n    Meta workflow editing gap: kspec meta set cannot update workflow steps. Agents must delete+recreate workflow to edit a step, which can churn workflow ULIDs and temporarily break references. Add safe step-level update support (e.g., --set-step <index> --content/--on-fail) or preserve ULID on replace to avoid reference drift.\n\n  01KJGW8Z (1 day ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, testing, cli]\n    Add stale-dist guard for CLI-based tests: detect outdated dist/cli build in tests/helpers/cli.ts and fail fast with actionable guidance (or optional auto-build) to avoid misleading failures.\n\n  01KJGFKJ (1 day ago by @claude) [cli, ux]\n    Unified kspec get @ref dispatch: currently plans require 'kspec plan get' while specs/tasks use 'kspec item get' / 'kspec task get'. A single 'kspec get @ref' that auto-dispatches to the right entity type would reduce friction for agents and humans who don't know the entity type upfront.\n\n  01KJGEEQ2Y (1 day ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, validation, traits]\n    Reflection follow-up: add validation check for trait/command-contract contradictions (e.g., command AC prohibits --refs while applied traits require batch refs semantics).\n\n  01KJGEEQ21 (1 day ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, ux]\n    Reflection follow-up: add optional ancestor-chain display for kspec item list --tree --search so matched descendants show parent context and avoid false orphan interpretation.\n\n  01KJGEEQ0 (1 day ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, spec]\n    Reflection follow-up: add explicit parent/ancestor context to kspec item get output (human and --json) so hierarchy is unambiguous without separate tree queries.\n\n  01KJGAE25 (1 day ago by @claude) [plan-import, automation, friction]\n    derive_from_specs produces sparse tasks: derived tasks get null description, no tags, automation unassessed. Agents picking up these tasks lack context to work independently. Consider: auto-populate description from spec description, copy plan tags, or allow plan document to specify per-task fields.\n\n  01KJGAE24 (1 day ago by @claude) [cli, tasks, friction]\n    task set --description: task set has no --description option, making it impossible to update task descriptions after creation. Workaround: add detailed task notes. Came up when plan-derived task had null description and needed standalone context for automation.\n\n  01KJGA18 (1 day ago by Jacob Chapel) [bug, infra, skills, setup]\n    Diagnose skill verify drift/no-hash for plugin-provided core skills.\n\nObserved behavior:\n- kspec skill verify reports codex statuses as drifted/no-hash for help, observations, triage, reflect, triage-inbox, writing-specs.\n- kspec skill diff reports plugin-provided (no local render to diff).\n- Rendered skill files exist under .agents/skills.\n\nInvestigate:\n- render-hash semantics in .kspec/skills for plugin-provided skills\n- plugin-provided status handling in skill verify/status/diff paths\n- whether this is expected behavior or a bug in drift detection\n\n  01KJG5A7 (1 day ago by Jacob Chapel) [bug, ralph, codex, sessions]\n    Task budget false-positive outside ralph loop when stale KSPEC_SESSION_ID is present (observed in codex session). Repro: with KSPEC_SESSION_ID set to an old session that has budget.json started_this_cycle=max_per_cycle, 'kspec task start @ref' fails with 'Task budget exhausted ... let iteration end naturally' even when not running ralph and no in-progress tasks. Expected: budget guard should apply only during active ralph loop iteration context, or stale/non-ralph session IDs should be ignored.\n\n  01KJF69V (2 days ago by @claude) [cli, validation]\n    kspec validate should warn about items with identical titles in the same module — two items both named 'Generation Progress UX' with different ULIDs caused confusion during a fix session in imgen project\n\n  01KJDZBA (2 days ago by @claude) [testing, ralph, infra]\n    Add second mock adapter registration for ralph tests. Currently --adapter-cmd registers a single 'custom' adapter, making it impossible to test different adapter IDs in real (non-dry-run) runs. Options: (1) --worker-adapter-cmd / --reviewer-adapter-cmd flags, (2) env-based adapter registration (e.g. KSPEC_TEST_ADAPTERS JSON), (3) allow --adapter-cmd to accept an optional name prefix like --adapter-cmd 'worker:node mock.js'. This would enable proper integration tests for ac-2 (reviewer subagent uses reviewer adapter), ac-7 (env injection for both adapters with different harness targets), and ac-12 (different adapter IDs in session metadata). Currently these are covered by code-level AC annotations and dry-run output tests, but lack end-to-end verification with the mock ACP.\n\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"124\nInbox (20 items):\n\n  01KJKY1H (8 hours ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, bug, batch, shadow]\n    Reflection follow-up: kspec batch can fail during copy-back with ENOTEMPTY on .kspec/sessions/<session-id> (observed while batching task start+note). The batch reports all subcommands succeeded, but exits non-zero and leaves shadow worktree in a temporarily broken/deleted-file state until manual recovery (e.g., git -C .kspec restore .). Improve batch copy-back robustness for session blob directories and avoid failing the batch when payload writes succeeded.\n\n  01KJKKCB (11 hours ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, tasks]\n    Reflection: add file-based payload input to task notes (e.g., kspec task note @ref --file <path> and/or --stdin). Current command only accepts positional message, which makes multiline/special-character notes brittle and conflicts with the convention to use file-based payloads for safe shell handling.\n\n  01KJKDBK (13 hours ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, testing, sessions]\n    tests/cli/session-note-limit.test.ts has reproducible timeout instability in current baseline. During this iteration: full npm test timed out at lines 34/70/104, and isolated run timed out at lines 34/104 despite rebuilding. This appears separate from ralph prompt changes and indicates session-start note-limit path/perf regression or test flake that should be stabilized.\n\n  01KJKC9T (13 hours ago by @claude) [fix, shadow, ralph]\n    Shadow branch auto-push silently swallows failures — fire-and-forget push (shadow.ts:1159-1164) uses spawn with stdio:ignore and detached:true, making divergence errors completely invisible. In a 4-hour ralph container session, 50+ commits accumulated locally with no indication pushes were failing. The push fails because host and container both commit to kspec-meta concurrently, causing divergence on the first push attempt, and every subsequent auto-push also fails silently. Fix should: (1) capture push exit code even in fire-and-forget mode (spawn with stdio:pipe, read stderr, unref after), (2) on push failure, log warning to stderr so it appears in agent output and JSON mode, (3) consider returning a result from autoCommit/shadowPush indicating push status so callers can surface it (e.g. kspec batch could include push_failed:true in response), (4) on repeated failures, escalate visibility — e.g. after 3 consecutive push failures, emit a prominent warning. The local commit always succeeds regardless — this is about VISIBILITY of sync failures, not blocking commits. Related: the merge driver handles YAML conflicts well (56/57 commits rebased cleanly), so the recovery path works — the problem is purely that nobody knows recovery is needed.\n\n  01KJK84Y (15 hours ago by @claude) [reflection, cli, tasks]\n    Reflection: fix-cycle ergonomics mismatch — task-work guidance says 're-submit' needs_work tasks, but CLI rejects kspec task submit when status is needs_work and requires explicit kspec task start first. Consider allowing direct submit from needs_work (auto-transition to in_progress) or improving error/help text to show required two-step command sequence.\n\n  01KJK5XG (15 hours ago by @claude) [reflection, testing, ralph]\n    tests/ralph.test.ts helper runRalph splits args on whitespace, so quoted values (e.g., --focus \"keep scope narrow\") break and cause misleading unknown-command/option failures. Improve helper to accept argv arrays or robust shell-like parsing for test args.\n\n  01KJK3YZ (16 hours ago by @claude) [reflection, git, automation]\n    Add commit-scope guard for automated task work: before committing, detect pre-existing staged files unrelated to the current task and prevent accidental inclusion (or require explicit acknowledgment). In this iteration, unrelated staged files were included because they were already in the index before git add on task files.\n\n  01KJJHAR (21 hours ago by @claude) [infra, cli, reflection]\n    Worktree guard escape hatch: add env var KSPEC_GUARD_OVERRIDE_DONT_USE that bypasses the guard hook for intentional recovery operations (git reset, rebase in .kspec). Name deliberately long to discourage agent use — humans-only for recovery scenarios.\n\n  01KJHAFV (1 day ago by @claude) [reflection, cli, workflow]\n    Meta workflow editing gap: kspec meta set cannot update workflow steps. Agents must delete+recreate workflow to edit a step, which can churn workflow ULIDs and temporarily break references. Add safe step-level update support (e.g., --set-step <index> --content/-